Crumb is a 1994 American documentary film about
the noted underground cartoonist R.

13
01:22:06,315 --> 01:22:12,315
Crumb and his family (including his two brothers) and his outlook on life.

14
01:28:25,263 --> 01:28:31,263
Directed by Terry Zwigoff and produced by Lynn
O'Donnell, it won widespread acclaim.

15
01:34:44,210 --> 01:34:50,210
It was released on the film festival circuit in September 1994 before being released theatrically in the United States on
April 28, 1995, having been screened at film festivals (and winning the Documentary Prize at Sundance) that year.

16
01:41:03,157 --> 01:41:09,157
Anderson (later critic for the San Francisco Examiner) placed the film on his list of the ten greatest films of all time, labeling it
"the greatest documentary ever made." The Criterion Collection released the film on DVD and Blu-ray on August 10, 2010.

17
01:47:22,105 --> 01:47:28,105
The film explores themes of underground, san
francisco, california, fetish, artist, nerd.

